THE AI RACE ELON MUSK CANNOT LOSE

Competitive Agitation, Vertical Integration, and the New Pipes Beneath the Machine Economy

Classification: Existential Threat — Premium Connectivity Required

Musk now occupies several parts of the same loop:

X
Attention, outrage, distribution, live data and advertising
Grok
AI subscriptions, API access and product integration
Colossus
Training and compute infrastructure
Tesla
Vehicles and future machine endpoints
Starlink
Global, mobile and remote connectivity
SpaceX
Launch capability and deployment of the network

SpaceX’s filing says AI-segment revenue is already coming from advertising, Grok and X subscriptions, and data-licensing arrangements. The same filing says Starlink had approximately 10.3 million subscriber lines by March 2026, while the Connectivity segment generated $4.423 billion in operating income during 2025. 

Grok is already inside compatible Tesla vehicles, where it requires either Wi-Fi or Tesla Premium Connectivity. So the “AI product connected to the paid communications layer inside the company’s physical endpoint” is not speculative—it is already a product arrangement. 

And xAI’s Colossus system is not a playful side project. The company describes a 200,000-H100 interconnected cluster and has claimed more than one million H100-equivalent units across its infrastructure by the end of 2025. 

The argument practically writes itself:

\text{AI alarm}
\rightarrow
\text{public attention}
\rightarrow
\text{competitive escalation}
\rightarrow
\text{more models, agents and devices}
\rightarrow
\text{more compute, connectivity and distribution}

Musk does not necessarily need Grok to become the sole winner.

He needs the race to continue.

If Grok wins, he owns the model.

If Grok remains competitive, he sells subscriptions, APIs and compute.

If AI becomes embedded in vehicles and physical machines, Tesla becomes an endpoint.

If AI becomes ubiquitous across remote, mobile and poorly connected environments, Starlink becomes more valuable.

If Starlink needs expansion, SpaceX launches the satellites.

If everyone wants to argue about it, the argument happens on X.

Whether that was all conceived as one master plan is unknowable and unnecessary. The observable incentive structure rewards him for keeping every participant frightened, excited, angry and accelerating.

One precision worth keeping in the paper: Starlink is not the backbone carrying traffic between most terrestrial AI data centres; that work is overwhelmingly fibre. The stronger and accurate “new pipes” argument is that Musk owns a rapidly expanding global access, mobile, direct-to-cell and remote-connectivity layer, plus the launch capability that places it in orbit. SpaceX began deploying Direct-to-Cell satellites specifically to eliminate conventional mobile dead zones. 

So he does not need to own every packet.

He needs AI to escape the data centre and proliferate into:

* cars;
* robots;
* ships;
* aircraft;
* farms;
* factories;
* military systems;
* remote infrastructure;
* and billions of permanently connected agents.

That is where his position becomes stronger than the ordinary “AI founder” position. Altman needs OpenAI to win. Amodei needs Anthropic to win. Zuckerberg needs Meta’s ecosystem to win.

Musk owns a horse, the starting pistol, the grandstand, the betting shop, the road to the stadium and the social network where everyone screams about the result.

Then he stands beside the track shouting that horse racing may destroy civilisation.

The opening could be:

Elon Musk’s public relationship with artificial intelligence resembles a man repeatedly pulling a fire alarm inside a shop that sells extinguishers, emergency communications, surveillance equipment and tickets to watch the building burn. He warns that AI may destroy civilisation, demands that competitors pause, founds a competing laboratory months later, builds one of the world’s largest compute installations, integrates its product into his social network and vehicles, and finally folds the entire operation into the company that owns the rockets and satellite network. Perhaps every warning is sincere. Sincerity does not alter the commercial effect. Every alarm increases attention. Every argument intensifies the race. Every new model creates more demand for compute, distribution, endpoints and connectivity. Musk does not need to know who wins the AI race. He has arranged matters so that the race itself is the product.

That is the one. Not Musk the AI prophet—Musk the race promoter who owns half the venue.
